g": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-16T20:52:24Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#29533634781", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Political party in north macedonia. Social Democratic Union of Macedonia is a political party in Skopje, established in 1991-04-20. A moderately significant entity in the historical record. Relevant to cause-and-effect analysis.", "newValue": "The Social Democratic Union of Macedonia (SDUM) is a political party established on April 20, 1991, in Skopje, North Macedonia, amidst the turbulent post-Yugoslav era. As a major player in the political landscape of North Macedonia, the SDUM emerged from the ashes of Socialist Yugoslavia, aiming to navigate the transition to a market economy while promoting social democracy. Key figures in its formation included Tito Petkovski, who served as the party's president, guiding the SDUM through the complexities of national identity and democratic governance during a time of significant change.

In the years following its establishment, the SDUM played a pivotal role in shaping the political discourse of North Macedonia. The party participated in the first multi-party elections in 1994, gaining a foothold in the national assembly, and subsequently held power in coalition governments. The SDUM was instrumental in the adoption of significant reforms aimed at aligning North Macedonia with European Union standards, especially in areas concerning human rights and minority protections. Their policies contributed to the stabilization of the country during crises, particularly during the ethnic tensions of the early 2000s.

